  "worked_solution": "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=3-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3(1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{\u2033}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=-6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(-1) & =6>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
